Why s2 is needed
The second stack temporarily reverses the elements.

For example:

s1:  1 2 3
       ↓
move to s2
       ↓
s2:  3 2 1

This reversal allows us to insert the new element while eventually restoring the correct queue order in s1.

Complexity
push()
We potentially move every existing element:

s1 → s2 → s1

So:

Time: O(n)

pop()
Only one stack operation:

Time: O(1)

peek()
Time: O(1)

empty()
Time: O(1)

Space
Two stacks together store all n elements:

Space: O(n)

So:

Time:
push  → O(n)
pop   → O(1)
peek  → O(1)
empty → O(1)

Space → O(n)

CODE-

class MyQueue {
private:
    stack<int> s1;
    stack<int> s2;

public:
    MyQueue() {}

    void push(int x) {
        while (!s1.empty()) {
            s2.push(s1.top());
            s1.pop();
        }
        s1.push(x);
        while (!s2.empty()) {
            s1.push(s2.top());
            s2.pop();
        }
    }

    int pop() {
        int temp = s1.top();
        s1.pop();
        return temp;
    }

    int peek() {
        return s1.top();
    }

    bool empty() {
        return s1.empty();
    }
};

